{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Cojocaru2004-QW-periodic-N48-npa02089",
  "Source": "E. Cojocaru, \"Quarter-wave discrete wavelet design of dichroic highly reflecting-transmitting mirrors for ultrafast solid-state lasers,\" Appl. Opt. 43, 1261-1265 (2004). doi:10.1364/AO.43.001261 - Sec. 4, p. 1263: periodic QW structure, na = 1.9, N = 48, npa = 0.2089 (n1 = 2.1089, n2 = 1.6911), n0 = ns = na",
  "Notes": "Periodic QW stack quoted in Sec. 4 as reaching R0 = 0.9999 at na = 1.9, N = 48 when npa = 0.2089 (zero-find of the periodic-stack R0 relation, Eq. 6)\nSpecification: R0 = 0.9999 at lambda0 with the smallest npa for N = 48.\nReported: Sec. 4: 'With a periodic QW structure, one achieves that R0 at na = 1.9 and N = 48 when npa = 0.2089', i.e. R0 = 0.9999 at lambda0 (left-hand dashed curve 2 of Fig. 4; Eq. 6). Analytic check: tanh^2[24 ln(2.1089/1.6911)] = 0.99990012. Stop-band width at R = 0.5 from Eq. (4): dl/l0 ~ (4/pi)(0.2089/1.9) = 0.140.\nNotes: lambda0 not stated for this example; 1000 nm used (R0 at lambda0 does not depend on it). Whether H or L is first is irrelevant to R with n0 = ns. Normal incidence; incident medium and substrate both n = na = 1.9 (n0 = ns = na), non-dispersive and lossless, so R is the same whichever side faces the light.\nLayers from Sec. 4, p. 1263: periodic QW structure, na = 1.9, N = 48, npa = 0.2089 (n1 = 2.1089, n2 = 1.6911), n0 = ns = na; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours R0 0.99990012 at 1000 nm; paper R0 = 0.9999 (Sec. 4).",
